'''Wairarapa''' is a New Zealand parliamentary [[New Zealand electorates|electorate]]. It was first created in 1858 (with the first election in 1859) and existed until 1881. It was recreated in 1887 and has since existed continuously. The current Wairarapa electorate MP is [[Mike Butterick]].

==Population centres==
The initial 24 New Zealand electorates were defined by Governor [[George Grey]] in March 1853, based on the [[New Zealand Constitution Act 1852]] that had been passed by the British government. The Constitution Act also allowed the [[New Zealand House of Representatives|House of Representatives]] to establish new electorates, and this was first done in 1858, when four new electorates were formed by splitting existing electorates.{{sfn|McRobie|1989|p=29}} Wairarapa was one of those four electorates, and it was established by splitting the {{NZ electorate link|Wairarapa and Hawke's Bay}} electorate, and incorporating areas that previously did not belong to any electorate.{{sfn|McRobie|1989|pp=28f}} Settlements in the initial area were [[Featherston, New Zealand|Featherston]], [[Carterton, New Zealand|Carterton]], [[Eketāhuna]], and [[Pahiatua]].{{sfn|McRobie|1989|p=32}} For the 1860 election, there were 266 voters registered.{{sfn|McRobie|1989|p=33}}In the early years, the electorate was for a time represented by two members.

In the 1887 electoral redistribution, although the [[Electoral Commission (New Zealand)|Representation Commission]] was required through the Representation Act 1887 to maintain existing electorates "as far as possible", rapid population growth in the [[North Island]] required the transfer of three seats from the [[South Island]] to the north. Ten new electorates were created, and one former electorate, Wairarapa, was recreated.{{sfn|McRobie|1989|pp=50–53}}

The electorate boundaries were last adjusted in the 2007 redistribution.<ref>{{cite book| title=Report of the Representation Commission 2007 |url= http://www.elections.org.nz/sites/default/files/2007%20Representation%20Commission%20Report.pdf |isbn=978-0-477-10414-2 |publisher=Representation Commission |access-date=4 October 2014 |page=9|date=14 September 2007}}</ref> No boundary adjustments were undertaken in the subsequent 2013/14 or 2019/20 redistributions.<ref>{{cite book| title=Report of the Representation Commission 2014 |url= http://www.elections.org.nz/sites/default/files/bulk-upload/documents/report_of_the_representation_commission_2014.pdf |isbn=978-0-477-10414-2 |publisher=Representation Commission |access-date=4 October 2014 |page=9|date=4 April 2014}}</ref>

