During the 2019/20 boundary review done by the [[Electoral Commission (New Zealand)|Electoral Commission]], [[Kieran McAnulty]], a [[List MP]] based in the electorate, and Central Hawke's Bay Mayor Alex Walker, proposed that the electorate be renamed to Wairarapa and Central Hawke's Bay in order to acknowledge the communities included in the electorate.<ref>{{cite web| url=https://elections.nz/assets/objection-form-uploads/09-01-20-Representation-Commission-signed-joint-letter-with-Alex-Walker-Change-name-of-electorate.pdf | access-date=2023-11-20 | title=Submission to the electorate boundary review - change of name of Wairarapa electorate}}</ref> Ultimately the commission decided against changing the name.

During the 2025 boundary review done by the [[Electoral Commission (New Zealand)|Electoral Commission]], the electorate would gain [[Ashhurst]] in the [[Manawatū-Whanganui]] region.<ref>{{cite web |date=8 August 2025 |title=Electorate boundaries finalised |url=https://elections.nz/media-and-news/2025/electorate-boundaries-finalised/// |work=Elections NZ}}</ref>

==History==
The first election was held on 7 November 1859, and [[Charles Carter (New Zealand politician)|Charles Carter]] was the first representative until 1865, when he resigned.{{sfn|Wilson|1985|p=188}}{{sfn|Wilson|1985|p=188}} He was succeeded by [[Henry Bunny]] from 1865 to 1881.{{sfn|Wilson|1985|p=187}} Since 1871, the electorate had two representatives,{{sfn|Wilson|1985|p=274}} and the second one was [[John Chapman Andrew]] until he resigned in 1877, succeeded by [[George Beetham]] from 1877 to 1881.{{sfn|Wilson|1985|pp=180, 183}}

From 1881 to 1887 Wairarapa was replaced by two electorates; [[Wairarapa North]] and [[Wairarapa South]]. From 1887, they were replaced by the [[Masterton (New Zealand electorate)|Masterton]] and Wairarapa electorates.{{sfn|Wilson|1985|pp=266–267, 274}}

Between 1899 and 1919 the Wairarapa electorate swung between [[Walter Clarke Buchanan]] the Conservative then [[Reform Party (New Zealand)|Reform]] candidate and [[J. T. Marryat Hornsby]] the [[New Zealand Liberal Party|Liberal]] candidate, changing hands in 1902, 1905, 1908 and 1914.{{sfn|Wilson|1985|pp=186, 206}}  Buchanan's support was in the rural areas, and Hornsby's was in the small towns.

From 1919 to 1928 the electorate was represented by [[Alex McLeod (politician)|Alex McLeod]] for Reform.{{sfn|Wilson|1985|p=217}} In 1928 he was defeated by [[Thomas William McDonald]] the United ([[New Zealand Liberal Party|Liberal]]) candidate, but in 1931 McLeod won the seat back.{{sfn|Wilson|1985|pp=214, 217}}

[[Ben Roberts (politician)|Ben Roberts]] represented the electorate for the [[New Zealand Labour Party|Labour Party]] from the {{NZ election link|1935}} until 1946, when he retired.{{sfn|Wilson|1985|p=201}} In the {{NZ election link|1943}}, Roberts was unsuccessfully challenged by [[New Zealand National Party|National]]'s [[Jimmy Maher (politician)|Jimmy Maher]].{{sfn|Gustafson|1986|p=332}}

In the {{NZ election link|1987}}, [[Reg Boorman]] won the initial count by one vote, but [[Wyatt Creech]] later challenged that result on the grounds that Boorman had violated new laws about election spending. Creech also challenged more than 200 votes (on various grounds). The Electoral Court upheld Creech's petition, and Creech won the seat in 1988 with a majority of 34 votes (9994 to 9960).<ref>{{cite news |last1=Tunnah |first1=Helen |title=Winston Peters threatens court bid |url= http://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/news/article.cfm?c_id=1&objectid=10345792 |access-date=10 October 2014 |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |date=16 September 2005}}</ref>

Creech held the Wairarapa electorate for four parliamentary terms. In December 1997, he became Deputy Leader of the National Party. That gave him number two on the National party list, and he did not contest an electorate in the {{NZ election link|1999}}. The National Party stood [[Paul Henry (broadcaster)|Paul Henry]] in the election, but to the general surprise of political commentators, the typically right-leaning electorate was won by Labour's [[Georgina Beyer]] with a 3,033 vote majority to become the world's first [[Transsexualism|transsexual]] member of parliament.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Trevett |first1=Claire |title=Georgina Beyer joins Mana Party |url= http://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/news/article.cfm?c_id=1&objectid=11299918 |access-date=10 October 2014 |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |date=27 July 2014}}</ref> At the {{NZ election link|2002}}, Beyer was easily re-elected with an increased majority of 6,372 votes.<ref>{{cite web |url= http://www.electionresults.govt.nz/electionresults_2002/electorate-56.html |title= Official Count Results – Wairarapa |publisher=[[Electoral Commission (New Zealand)|Electoral Commission]] |date= 10 August 2002 |access-date= 27 December 2011}}</ref> Beyer stood in the {{NZ election link|2005}} as a list-only candidate, and the Wairarapa electorate was won by [[John Hayes (New Zealand politician)|John Hayes]] of the National Party.<ref name="Hayes parliamentary profile">{{cite web| title=John Hayes |url= http://www.parliament.nz/en-nz/mpp/mps/former/50PlibMpsFormerJohnHayes1/hayes-john |publisher=[[New Zealand Parliament]] |access-date=10 October 2014 |date=22 September 2014}}</ref>

Hayes held the electorate for three parliamentary terms and retired at the {{NZ election link|2014}},<ref name="Hayes parliamentary profile" /><ref>{{cite news| url= http://www.stuff.co.nz/dominion-post/news/wairarapa/9623424/Wairarapa-MP-Hayes-calls-time |title=Wairarapa MP Hayes calls time |first=Vernon |last=Small |author-link=Vernon Small |date=18 January 2014 |access-date=20 September 2014 |publisher=[[The Dominion Post (Wellington)|The Dominion Post]]}}</ref> when he was succeeded by National's [[Alastair Scott (politician)|Alastair Scott]].<ref>{{cite web |title=Official Count Results – Wairarapa |url= http://electionresults.org.nz/electionresults_2014/electorate-58.html |publisher=[[Electoral Commission (New Zealand)|Electoral Commission]] |access-date=10 October 2014 |date=10 October 2014}}</ref> Scott retired at the 2020 election. The seat was won by [[Kieran McAnulty]] in a surprise Labour Party swing, with new National candidate Mike Butterick taking second place. Mike Butterick won the seat at the 2023 election and is the current Wairarapa MP. McAnulty returned to Parliament via the Labour Party list.{{citation needed|date=November 2023}}

